NAME
       nice - start a program with a different scheduling priority

SYNOPSIS
       nice [-n ADJUSTMENT] COMMAND [ARGUMENT...]
       nice

DESCRIPTION
       Every process has a nice value from -20 to 19. It tells the scheduler
       how generous the process is towards others: a high nice value (up to
       19) means "let the others go first", a low or negative one means "give
       me more of the CPU". New processes inherit the nice value of the shell
       that starts them, normally 0. The value only matters when the CPU is
       busy: an idle CPU runs everything at full speed.

       nice starts COMMAND with its nice value raised by ADJUSTMENT (10 when
       -n is not given). Without a command, it prints the current nice value.

       Anyone can make their own processes nicer (a higher value). Only root
       can lower the value, below 0 included: for a normal user nice warns
       and runs the command anyway with the value unchanged.

       To change the nice value of a process that is already running, use
       renice. ps and top show the value in the NI column.

OPTIONS
       -n, --adjustment=N
              Add N to the nice value. N can be negative (root only). The
              result is kept between -20 and 19.

       -N     Old short form of -n N, such as nice -5 (meaning +5) or
              nice --5 (meaning -5). Prefer -n, which is clearer.

EXAMPLES
       Run a long compression job without slowing down the rest of the
       system:

           nice -n 19 tar -czf /root/backup.tar.gz /home

       Start a job with nice value 5 in the background, then check it:

           nice -n 5 sha256sum /dev/zero &
           ps -o pid,ni,comm -C sha256sum

       As root, start a program with a higher priority (nice -10):

           nice -n -10 /usr/local/bin/important-job

       Show the nice value of the current shell:

           nice

NOTES
       Exam tasks sometimes say "priority" when they mean the nice value. ps
       -l shows both: PRI (the kernel's priority, derived from the nice
       value) and NI (the nice value you set).

SEE ALSO
       renice(1), ps(1), top(1), kill(1), tuned-adm(8)
